# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Revisions: Section 5 (Theoretical Framework), part A: 5.1-5.4

REVS = {
    # 5.1
    "P147": ("A theoretical framework is a set of assumptions and concepts,",
             "A theoretical framework is a set of assumptions and concepts, connected by an argument, that determines what a study observes, how it interprets what it observes, and what it will accept as an explanation. It is not a summary of theories that seem relevant; it is the structure that makes the study coherent, and it does three kinds of work at once. It specifies an ontology, a view of what exists and what kinds of things can act; an epistemology, a view of how knowledge about those things can be produced; and an axiology, a view of what the research is for and whose interests it serves."),
    "P148": ("The literature on doctoral supervision is consistent",
             "The literature on doctoral research is consistent about this function. Grant and Osanloo (2014) describe the theoretical framework as the blueprint of a dissertation: it determines the rooms that can be built and the connections between them. Ravitch and Riggan (2017) argue that a conceptual framework is not a diagram added at the end of a literature review but the researcher's reasoned account of why the study is designed as it is. Sandberg and Alvesson (2011) show that the construction of a research question is itself a theoretical act, because the question already assumes a way of dividing the world, and Alvesson and K\u00e4rreman (2007) add that empirical material becomes interesting when it resists the theory that was used to generate it, not when it confirms it."),
    "P149": ("Four functions follow from this.",
             "Four functions follow. First, a framework prevents description from masquerading as analysis: a study that reports what people said, without a theory of why they said it, produces data rather than knowledge. Second, it makes assumptions contestable \u2014 if a study assumes that households are rational choosers, that assumption can be examined and, if necessary, rejected, whereas an implicit assumption cannot be examined at all. Third, it connects the study to a tradition, so that the reader can see which conversation is being joined and therefore what counts as a contribution. Fourth, it disciplines interpretation: a researcher who has committed to a theory can be held to its implications, while a researcher who has not can explain any finding after the fact."),
    "P150": ("The relationship between theory and empirical work runs in both directions.",
             "The relationship between theory and empirical work runs in both directions. Theory shapes the questions, the design and the interpretation, and empirical material can in turn revise the theory \u2014 the logic of abduction, which moves between a theoretical frame and a surprising observation (Alvesson and K\u00e4rreman, 2007). The surprise is not a nuisance; it is the point at which the study has something to say."),
    "P151": ("A theoretical framework is not the same as a conceptual framework,",
             "A theoretical framework is not the same as a conceptual framework, and the distinction matters for the structure of this proposal. The theoretical framework is the general body of theory that the study adopts, together with the justification for adopting it; it is abstract and portable. The conceptual framework is the researcher's own model of the specific problem, derived from theory, literature and context; it is concrete and local. Section 5 establishes the theory, and Section 6 constructs the model. A common weakness in applied research is to collapse the two, so that the model is presented as if it were a theory and the theory is never tested against anything."),
    "P152": ("The consequences of a weak or absent framework are predictable.",
             "The consequences of a weak or absent framework are predictable: variables are selected ad hoc, claims are made that cannot be falsified, findings cannot be generalised beyond the case because there is no theory to say what they are a case of, and recommendations follow the researcher's preferences rather than the analysis. In engineering management, where the pressure to be useful is strong, a framework is sometimes treated as an academic ornament that delays the practical work. The argument of this section is the opposite: without a framework, the practical work has no way of knowing what it is looking at."),

    # 5.2
    "P154": ("Engineering Management sits at the intersection",
             "Engineering Management sits at the intersection of technical systems, organisational behaviour, economics, innovation studies and public policy, and its problems are rarely confined to one level. A decision about a technology is also a decision about skills, contracts, maintenance, regulation and trust. A project that is technically sound can fail because the organisation that was supposed to operate it did not have the capacity, because the tariff structure made the service unaffordable, or because the community had learned from previous projects that promises are not kept."),
    "P155": ("The field has borrowed theories from several disciplines.",
             "The field has borrowed theories from several disciplines: the Technology Acceptance Model and its successors from psychology and information systems, Lean Thinking and the resource-based view from operations, complexity theory and systems thinking from complexity science, and socio-technical systems theory and the multi-level perspective from sociology and innovation studies. Each has been productive in the settings for which it was developed. The difficulty arises when a theory developed for a firm, an individual or a market is applied to an infrastructural problem in a low-income settlement: the theory does not simply lose precision, it mis-specifies the object."),
    "P156": ("Three mis-specifications recur.",
             "Three mis-specifications recur. The first is level confusion: a theory of individual intention is used to explain a collective outcome, or a theory of firm strategy to explain a household practice. The second is the black-boxing of technology: the artefact is treated as a fixed input whose properties are known, when its properties are part of what has to be explained. The third is the treatment of context as background \u2014 what the model controls for, rather than what the model is about. In a study of energy in an informal settlement, context is the substance: tenure, density, infrastructure, income, gender and municipal capacity are not variables to be held constant but the conditions that make the technology work or fail."),
    "P157": ("A framework adequate to this problem needs to be relational and multi-level.",
             "A framework adequate to this problem therefore needs to be relational and multi-level, to hold human and non-human elements in one analytical frame without reducing one to the other, to be normative because the problem is about justice and not only about efficiency, and to be empirically tractable because the study has to produce findings that can be defended. These four requirements are the criteria against which the theory is selected in Section 5.11."),
    "P158": ("There is also a question of disciplinary fit.",
             "There is also a question of disciplinary fit. Engineering management values usefulness, and a theory that cannot be used to make a decision will not survive in the field, however elegant it is. The framework developed here is therefore assessed not only for its philosophical coherence but for whether it changes what a design team would do on a Monday morning."),

    # 5.3.1
    "P161": ("Humanism is less a single doctrine than a family of commitments.",
             "Humanism is less a single doctrine than a family of commitments. It treats the human being as the central subject of knowledge and value, separates culture from nature and mind from body, and assumes that the human has a universal essence that is the measure against which other beings are judged. These commitments made possible important achievements \u2014 universal rights, scientific inquiry, the critique of arbitrary authority. They also produced exclusions: the universal human was, in practice, a particular kind of human \u2014 male, European, propertied, rational and able-bodied \u2014 and those who did not fit were treated as less than fully human, as nature rather than culture, or as objects of study rather than participants in knowledge."),
    "P162": ("Posthumanism begins from this diagnosis.",
             "Posthumanism begins from this diagnosis. It does not reject the achievements of humanism; it rejects the pretence that the human is a self-contained, universal subject, and asks what becomes visible when that pretence is dropped. The question is not \u201cwhat comes after the human?\u201d but \u201chow has the human been produced, and what else is involved in that production?\u201d"),

    # 5.3.2
    "P164": ("The term posthumanism covers several projects,",
             "The term posthumanism covers several projects, and the differences matter."),
    "P165": ("The first is posthumanism as a historical condition.",
             "The first is posthumanism as a historical condition. The human is increasingly entangled with technologies, data, infrastructures and ecological systems, and the boundaries between body and machine and between nature and culture are becoming harder to draw. This condition is described by Hayles (1999) and by Braidotti (2013), although they evaluate it differently."),
    "P166": ("The second is critical posthumanism.",
             "The second is critical posthumanism, a normative and philosophical project that draws on anti-humanism, feminism, postcolonial thought and science and technology studies. It affirms difference rather than universalism and treats knowledge as situated. Braidotti (2013, 2019) is its most systematic contemporary exponent, and Wolfe (2010) distinguishes it carefully from the third formation."),
    "P167": ("The third is transhumanism.",
             "The third is transhumanism, which seeks to enhance human capacities through technology: longer life, greater intelligence, control over the body. It is often described as posthuman, but its values remain humanist \u2014 it celebrates perfectibility, mastery and transcendence, and treats the body as a limitation to be overcome rather than as a condition of knowledge. This study rejects transhumanism, and the rejection is not a matter of taste: transhumanism would direct attention to the enhancement of individual capacities and away from the collective, material and institutional arrangements that are the subject of this study."),
    "P168": ("A fourth position, technological determinism, is also rejected.",
             "A fourth position, technological determinism, is also rejected. Determinism holds that technology drives social change according to its own logic. The alternative adopted here is not that society is autonomous, but that technology and society are co-constituted. A prepaid meter does not cause a particular relation between landlord and tenant; it materialises a relation that is already being negotiated, and in doing so it changes the terms of the negotiation."),

    # 5.3.3
    "P170": ("The central ontological claim of critical posthumanism",
             "The central ontological claim of critical posthumanism is that entities do not precede their relations. This is stated most rigorously by Barad (2007). In agential realism, the primary unit is the phenomenon, not the object: a phenomenon is an entanglement of matter and meaning in which boundaries are enacted, and the entities that appear within it \u2014 the relata \u2014 acquire their properties through intra-action. They do not exist first and then interact; intra-action is the relation through which they emerge."),
    "P171": ("This is a difficult claim, and it is easy to repeat without understanding it.",
             "This is a difficult claim, and an example may make it concrete. A biogas digester is not a thing with fixed properties that is then introduced into a household. What the digester is, in practice, depends on the feedstock that is available, the water that can be spared, the space that is allocated, the stove that is used, the person who maintains it and the agreements that govern it. Change the water supply and the digester becomes a different object: it may become a burden rather than a service. The properties that matter are not intrinsic to the vessel; they are produced in the relation."),
    "P172": ("Haraway's (1988) epistemology is a companion to this ontology.",
             "Haraway's (1988) epistemology is a companion to this ontology. Knowledge is always from somewhere: the idea of a \u201cview from nowhere\u201d is not a description of objectivity but a concealment of a particular position. Situated knowledge is not less objective; it is objective in a different way, because it can be located and therefore checked. For this study the implication is direct: the knowledge of a resident about how a stove behaves in a small kitchen is not anecdote to be validated by an expert. It is knowledge produced at a particular site, and it is indispensable to a design that has to work at that site."),

    # 5.3.4
    "P174": ("If entities emerge through relations, then agency",
             "If entities emerge through relations, then agency cannot be a property that belongs to humans alone. Barad (2007) describes agency as an enactment, not an attribute: it is distributed across the apparatus through which a phenomenon is observed and through the materials that participate in it. The agential cut is the boundary drawn within a phenomenon between subject and object, or between what is observed and what observes. The cut is not arbitrary, but it is a cut: it includes some things and excludes others, and it has consequences."),
    "P175": ("Latour's (2005) actor-network theory is adjacent to this position",
             "Latour's (2005) actor-network theory is adjacent to this position but not identical. Latour treats humans and non-humans symmetrically as actants and traces the networks through which action is assembled. His work is empirically useful because it shows how a technology enrols allies and how it can be undone, but less useful for normative questions, because it is deliberately indifferent to the justice of the network it describes. This study uses Latour's sensibility without adopting his ontology wholesale."),
    "P176": ("Bennett's (2010) notion of thing-power is also relevant.",
             "Bennett's (2010) notion of thing-power is also relevant. Bennett argues that matter has a vitality of its own, that things can resist, surprise and exceed human intention. A digester that produces too little gas, a pipe that clogs, a stove that will not sit flat on a pot: these are not merely technical failures but moments in which the material world asserts its own agency and changes what the household can do."),
    "P177": ("The consequence for research is significant.",
             "The consequence for research is significant. If agency is distributed, then a study that asks only what people think has already excluded most of what is going on: the meter, the fuel, the dwelling, the water supply and the maintenance arrangement. Those are not context. They are participants."),

    # 5.3.5
    "P179": ("Posthumanism takes matter seriously.",
             "Posthumanism takes matter seriously, which distinguishes it from social constructionism's willingness to treat the material world as a product of discourse. Matter is not passive: it has properties that enable some actions and constrain others. A prepaid meter, for example, is not a neutral device for measuring consumption. It enacts a particular relation between the household, the utility and money: it requires a purchase before use, it can disconnect a household automatically, and it shifts the management of cash flow onto the household. Those are material effects that shape practice, and they are not captured by asking whether the meter is easy to use."),
    "P180": ("A biogas digester is another example.",
             "A biogas digester is another example. Its material requirements \u2014 water, feedstock, temperature and space \u2014 produce a set of social requirements: someone must collect the waste, someone must carry the water, someone must be available when the system needs attention, and someone must be able to buy a spare part. The technology distributes labour, and the distribution is usually gendered. This is not a social consequence that follows from a technical artefact; it is a property of the artefact-in-relation."),

    # 5.3.6
    "P182": ("Posthumanism also insists on the interdependence",
             "Posthumanism also insists on the interdependence of human and ecological systems. The nature\u2013culture distinction is not a description of the world but a way of organising it, and that organisation has costs. The Anthropocene names a condition in which human activity has become a geological force, and in which the consequences of that activity are unevenly distributed: those who contributed least to emissions are often most exposed to their effects."),
    "P183": ("For this study, ecological interdependence matters in two ways.",
             "For this study, ecological interdependence matters in two ways. First, energy interventions have environmental effects that are not externalities to be added at the end: a biogas system that consumes water in a water-scarce settlement is not environmentally benign simply because it is renewable. Second, environmental justice and social justice are not separate agendas: the same arrangements that expose residents to smoke and fire also expose them to floods, heat and pollution. A framework that treats the social as one domain and the environmental as another will miss the connection."),

    # 5.3.7
    "P185": ("Posthumanist ethics is relational and affirmative.",
             "Posthumanist ethics is relational and affirmative. Braidotti (2006, 2013) contrasts potestas, a power that negates and constrains, with potentia, a capacity that enables and creates \u2014 a distinction that avoids the assumption that power is always repressive. A community can have the capacity to organise, to learn and to build even when it lacks formal authority, and the task of a just intervention is to increase potentia without ignoring the potestas that constrains it."),
    "P186": ("Haraway (2016) offers the concept of response-ability:",
             "Haraway (2016) offers the concept of response-ability: the capacity to respond to others, human and non-human, in a way that is accountable. Response-ability is not a rule but a practice of attention. Barad (2007) describes ethics as ethico-onto-epistemology, insisting that questions of being and knowing cannot be separated from questions of value: what we choose to observe, and how, is already an ethical decision."),
    "P187": ("These ideas have practical implications for a Living Lab.",
             "These ideas have practical implications for a Living Lab. Who is invited to the first meeting is an ethical decision. Which data are recorded and which are not is an ethical decision. Who is credited for an innovation, and who bears the risk of failure, are ethical decisions. A framework that treats ethics as a consent form to be signed at the start will miss most of them."),

    # 5.3.8 (table renumbered 3.2 -> 3.1 so tables appear in order within Section 5)
    "P189": ("The term is used loosely in popular writing,",
             "The term is used loosely in popular writing, and it is important to be precise. Table 3.1 distinguishes the position adopted here from several positions with which it is often confused."),
    "P190": ("Table 3.2: What posthumanism is not",
             "Table 3.1: What posthumanism is not"),

    # 5.4 (table renumbered 3.1 -> 3.2 so tables appear in order within Section 5)
    "P193": ("Critical posthumanism is not a single system.",
             "Critical posthumanism is not a single system. The four thinkers used in this study disagree about ontology, method and politics, and the disagreements are productive. Table 3.2 summarises their contributions and limits."),
    "P194": ("Table 3.1: Comparison of the four posthumanist thinkers",
             "Table 3.2: Comparison of the four posthumanist thinkers used in this study"),
    "P196": ("Braidotti supplies the normative horizon.",
             "Braidotti supplies the normative horizon. Her critique of humanist universalism is the reason the study cannot treat \u201cthe user\u201d as a generic category; her affirmative ethics is the reason the framework is oriented towards capacity-building rather than deficit-repair; and her distinction between potestas and potentia, developed in Transpositions (Braidotti, 2006) and used by Aboalez (2025), gives the study a way of talking about power that is not only negative. But Braidotti's work is not a method: it does not tell the researcher what to observe in a settlement, and it can encourage a style of writing that is more evocative than analytical. The study uses her concepts and resists her prose."),
    "P197": ("Haraway supplies the epistemology.",
             "Haraway supplies the epistemology. Her argument that knowledge is situated is the strongest available alternative to the assumption that the designer knows best, and her concept of response-ability describes the ethical relation between researchers and residents more demandingly than informed consent does. Her later work on sympoiesis, or making-with (Haraway, 2016), is directly relevant to a Living Lab, because it describes collaboration as a condition of life rather than as a technique. The limitation is that Haraway is deliberately unsystematic: she offers figures and provocations, not procedures."),
    "P198": ("Barad supplies the ontology and the analytical unit.",
             "Barad supplies the ontology and the analytical unit. Intra-action is the reason the study treats the phenomenon, rather than the user or the artefact, as the unit of analysis; the apparatus is the reason the Living Lab can be understood as producing the phenomenon it studies, rather than observing it from outside; and diffraction \u2014 the practice of reading insights through one another rather than against one another \u2014 is used in the analysis to hold the interview, observational and workshop material together. The risk is obscurity: Barad's vocabulary is drawn from quantum physics, and it can be used to impress rather than to explain. Every technical term in this proposal is therefore defined in plain English and illustrated with an energy example."),
    "P199": ("Hayles supplies the corrective to abstraction.",
             "Hayles supplies the corrective to abstraction. Her account of embodiment insists that information is always instantiated in a body, a device and a material arrangement, and her concept of technogenesis \u2014 the idea that humans and technologies co-evolve \u2014 supports the study's refusal to treat the technology as an external object. Her work is less useful for questions of justice and participation, and it is not asked to carry that weight."),
    "P200": ("The four thinkers converge on a rejection",
             "The four thinkers converge on a rejection of the autonomous humanist subject, on relationality, and on the critique of the nature\u2013culture divide. They diverge on almost everything else: Braidotti is a vitalist monist, Barad a realist about relations, Haraway a situated pragmatist, Hayles a media theorist of embodiment. The study does not present them as a single theory. It assembles a small number of concepts from each, states why each is needed, and accepts that the assembly is a construction rather than a discovery."),
    "P201": ("This is also the point at which the study connects",
             "This is also the point at which the study connects to the supervisor's work. Aboalez (2025) draws primarily on Braidotti and Barad to argue for a posthumanist social approach to energy transition. The present study extends that argument from engineering education to settlement-level design, and adds Haraway's epistemology and Hayles's materialism to address questions that the earlier paper did not have to answer."),
}

